**Real-World Attack Prevention**

Case Study 1: MaliciousCorgi Campaign
The scanner detected MaliciousCorgi (1.5M installs) by identifying:
- Hidden iframe: Zero-pixel iframe loading analytics SDKs
- File exfiltration: onDidOpenTextDocument reading entire files
- Base64 encoding: File contents encoded before transmission
- Remote command: getFilesList harvesting up to 50 files
- Analytics SDKs: Zhuge.io, GrowingIO, TalkingData, Baidu Analytics
- Domain blocklist: aihao123.cn

Case Study 2: Fake ClawdBot Trojan
Detected by flagging:
- Startup activation: onStartupFinished in manifest
- Dynamic fetch: Downloading from C2 server on activation
- ScreenConnect RAT: Known remote access tool signature
- Triple fallback: Multiple delivery mechanisms
- Process execution: child_process.exec() patterns
- Hidden batch script: PowerShell downloader fallback

**Extending the Scanner**
Adding Custom YARA Rules
Create custom_rules.yar:

rule custom_exfil_detection {
    meta:
        description = "Custom data exfiltration pattern"
        severity = "high"
    strings:
        $discord_webhook = /discord\.com\/api\/webhooks\/[0-9]+\/[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/
        $telegram_bot = /api\.telegram\.org\/bot[0-9]+:[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\/sendMessage/
        $suspicious_fetch = /fetch\s*\(\s*['"](https?:\/\/[^'"]+)/
    condition:
        any of them
}

Run with custom rules:

ai-plugin-scanner scan --yara-rules ./custom_rules.yar extension.vsix
Programmatic API
